  <prompt>
    Extend the existing communication architecture documentation in `docs/architecture/` to describe the messaging channel as-built, and wire in **ADR-046** (ACS messaging channel, authored in task 007). Use the `docs-architecture` skill.

    Prefer EXTENDING the canonical R4 substrate doc `docs/architecture/communication-intelligence-architecture.md` (and/or `communication-service-architecture.md`) rather than authoring a new standalone doc (§11 — the communication platform is ONE platform; messaging is its second channel, not a new subsystem). Add, grounded in the shipped code:

    - **The first-class thread data model** — `sprk_communicationthread` entity + `sprk_communication.sprk_thread` lookup + the thread↔channel child table (one row per `(thread, channel, external-ref)`; R1 populates ACS `ChatThreadId`); thread topologies (record-anchored via the ADR-024 regarding family + 1:1 direct); thread-level privacy state; point-forward switch.
    - **The `IThreadResolver`** (direction-symmetric find-or-create) — invoked by both inbound capture and outbound send, for all channels; email uses `sprk_inreplyto` ancestry, chat uses ACS `ChatThreadId`; the extension of `ThreadContinuityRung` (inbound) + `CommunicationService` (outbound); the "email conversations become grouped threads" feature (design §6.5).
    - **ACS transport** — ACS Chat is transport, Dataverse is the record; server-side identity mapping + token minting; thread create with 30-day retention; membership as a reconciled projection of Dataverse-derived access (ADR-034); NO client-side ACS SDK in R1 (polling timeline reads persisted records).
    - **The inbound ingestor seam** — the net-new `ICommunicationChannelIngestor` + `MessagingIngestor`; the Event Grid capture pipeline (webhook → Service Bus job → normalizer → `NormalizedMessage` → idempotent persist → enrichment); echo-dedup on ACS message id.
    - **Cross-reference ADR-046** (canonical ACS messaging-channel ADR) + related ADRs (045 channel seams, 024 regarding, 034 membership, 028 auth, 027 provisioning, 004/036 jobs, 007 SPE). Keep it a design/rationale doc (WHY + structure) with code entry-point pointers — no implementation dumps.
  </prompt>

  <role>
    Spaarke architecture writer authoritative on the Communication subsystem. Uses the `docs-architecture` skill and follows repo architecture-doc conventions (decisions + rationale + code entry-point pointers). Extends the canonical R4 communication docs rather than forking a new one.
  </role>

  <goal>
    The communication architecture doc(s) in `docs/architecture/` accurately describe the as-built messaging channel — thread data model, `IThreadResolver`, ACS transport, and the inbound ingestor seam — cross-referencing ADR-046 and related ADRs. The doc matches shipped code and is extended (not forked) from the canonical R4 substrate.
  </goal>

  <context>
    <background>
      email-r4 W8 authored `docs/architecture/communication-intelligence-architecture.md` as the canonical R4 substrate (absorbing the retired email-processing doc) and refreshed `communication-service-architecture.md`. R1 adds the SECOND channel + a NEW thread model + a NEW ingestor seam — the biggest as-built additions since R4. This task documents them by extending those docs, keeping ONE communication architecture narrative (§11: messaging is a channel, not a new subsystem).

      ADR-046 (task 007) is the canonical ACS messaging-channel ADR (concise + full). This doc is the architecture-level companion: it describes the delivered structure and rationale and points at ADR-046 for the codified decision, and at code entry points (thread resolver, ingestor, ACS folder) for implementation.

      Source-of-truth (root §2): verify each described behavior against shipped code (040 resolver, 021/030/031 ingestor + capture, 004/005 schema, 010/011 ACS) before documenting it. Do NOT document plan-only intentions.
    </background>
